ITEMS = ((('what should i do when someone faints', 'how do i treat a person who has fainted', 'what is the first aid for fainting', 'how to help someone who has fainted', 'what to do immediately after fainting', 'how do you handle a faint', 'what helps a person who faints', 'how can i assist after fainting', 'what is the best response to fainting', 'how should i care for someone who fainted'), 'If someone faints, ease them gently to the floor and support their head. Loosen tight clothing, check breathing and place them in the recovery position if they remain drowsy. Call emergency services if they do not wake quickly or seem unwell.'), (('what should i do when i feel faint', 'how to stop myself fainting', 'what helps prevent a faint', 'how to sit when feeling faint', 'what if i feel faint at work', 'how to react to warning signs of fainting', 'should i lie down if i feel faint', 'what to do before a faint happens', 'how to stay safe when dizzy after fainting', 'when to get help for feeling faint'), 'If you feel faint, sit or lie down, lower your head and loosen tight clothing. Avoid standing quickly and ask someone to stay with you. Tell a doctor if fainting happens often, as there may be an underlying cause to check.'), (('how long does fainting usually last', 'what if fainting lasts a long time', 'should i wake someone after fainting', 'what if the person is confused after a faint', 'how to check someone after fainting', 'what if fainting happens twice', 'how to watch someone who has fainted', 'what to ask after a faint', 'when to call help for prolonged fainting', 'who should check after fainting'), 'Most faints are brief, with recovery over a few minutes. Let the person rest and check that they are alert and breathing normally. Call emergency services if they stay unconscious, seem confused, or the faint follows an injury.'), (('can heat cause fainting', 'what to do for fainting in hot weather', 'how to cool someone who faints in the sun', 'is fainting a sign of heat exhaustion', 'what to do if a person faints outdoors', 'how to move someone after fainting in heat', 'what if fainting happens during exercise', 'how to prevent fainting in hot rooms', 'when to call help for heat related fainting', 'who can advise about fainting in heat'), 'Heat can lead to fainting, so move the person to a cool shaded place and loosen clothing. Give water only when they are fully awake. Call emergency services if they remain drowsy, have hot dry skin or seem confused.'), (('what if someone faints after an injury', 'should i check for injuries after fainting', 'can a head injury cause fainting', 'how to handle a fall with fainting', 'what if fainting follows a blow to the head', 'should i move someone who fainted and fell', 'how to check breathing after fainting from injury', 'what if the person cannot remember the fall after fainting', 'when to call an ambulance after fainting and injury', 'who can assess an injury after fainting'), 'After a fall or head injury, treat the person as possibly hurt and call emergency services. Do not move them unless they are in danger, and check breathing. A doctor should assess any head injury with fainting, even if the person feels fine.'), (('what to do when a child faints', 'can babies faint', 'how to help a young person who faints', 'should i call a doctor for a child who faints', 'how to place a child after fainting', 'what symptoms after fainting need review', 'how to reassure a child after a faint', 'what if a teenager faints at school', 'when to call emergency services for child fainting', 'who should examine a child after fainting'), 'Lay a child down and check breathing after a faint, then let them rest. Tell a doctor or parent what happened. Call emergency services if the child does not wake promptly, seems unwell, or the faint followed an injury.'), (('what should i do after waking from fainting', 'how to feel after a faint after fainting', 'can i stand up straight away after fainting', 'what to eat or drink after fainting', 'should i rest after fainting', 'what if i feel sick after fainting', 'how to recover at home after fainting', 'when to drive after fainting', 'what if fainting happens again after fainting', 'who can advise about recovery from fainting'), 'After waking, rest for a while and stand up slowly, with someone nearby if possible. Drink water once you are fully alert. Ask a doctor if fainting recurs or you feel unwell afterwards, and avoid driving until you have been advised.'), (('when should i call an ambulance for fainting', 'is fainting always an emergency', 'what if someone does not wake after fainting', 'should i call for a brief faint', 'what symptoms with fainting need urgent help', 'how to decide about calling after fainting', 'what if fainting happens with chest pain', 'when to seek help during fainting', 'what to tell the operator about a faint', 'who decides if fainting needs an ambulance'), 'Call an ambulance if the person does not wake quickly, has trouble breathing, chest pain, confusion, or fainted after an injury. Tell the operator how long the faint lasted and what happened before. Stay with the person until help arrives.'), (('what causes fainting spells', 'can low blood sugar lead to fainting', 'is dehydration a cause of fainting', 'can stress cause fainting', 'what medical conditions cause fainting', 'can standing too long cause fainting', 'what if fainting happens without warning', 'how to find the cause of fainting', 'when to ask a doctor about causes after fainting', 'who can investigate repeated fainting'), 'Fainting can follow dehydration, heat, low blood sugar, standing for long periods or a heart problem. A doctor can investigate if it is repeated or unexplained. Call emergency services for a faint with other serious symptoms.'), (('should i give water after fainting', 'can i give food to someone who has fainted', 'what to give a person who faints', 'should i give sugar after fainting', 'is it safe to give tablets after fainting', 'how to help someone sit up after fainting', 'should i loosen clothing after fainting', 'what if the person is still drowsy after fainting', 'when to offer a drink after fainting', 'who can advise about giving things after fainting'), 'Do not give food, drink or tablets while the person is drowsy, as they may choke. Once fully alert, water can be offered slowly. Call emergency services if consciousness does not return quickly or the person seems confused.'))
